            <div xml:id="d73">

                <pb facs="0169" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1935v01/pg67"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15A5/395a: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1935-03-26T19:00:00-05:00">March 26, 1935—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>49. 1. The Embassy at London reports for our strictly confidential information
                    that:</p>
                <p>[Here follows the text of the last two paragraphs of the Embassy’s telegram No.
                    121, March 19, 7 p.m., <hi rendition="#italic">supra</hi>.]</p>
                <p>2. The Department has received a report from a reliable source that during the
                    week following the promulgation by Germany of the new conscription law the
                    Japanese Ambassador in Berlin had conversed three times with high officials of
                    the more aggressive element in the German Cabinet, and that Germany is being
                    pressed by Japan to ask for restoration of naval strength.</p>
                <p>3. All of the foregoing is for your strictly confidential information and for any
                    comment you may be in a position to offer. Please make no inquiry in any
                    quarter.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
